Program only the flash bytes that change: a variable reclaim no longer
holds a boot for most of a minute.

Our own patch. OVMF's QemuFlashWrite programs every byte it is given,
each as two writes to QEMU's pflash device: the program command, then
the byte. Under KVM both writes leave the guest, and QEMU's pflash_cfi01
(8.2.2, and master on 2026-10-25) writes the byte's 512-byte sector of
the varstore file synchronously from the vCPU thread before the guest
runs on: 47-69 us a byte on this project's host, idle (docs/decisions/0032).

That is harmless for the few kilobytes a boot writes, and not for a
reclaim. OVMF's own boot manager rewrites ConOut, ConIn and ErrOut 14
times every boot (it adds its platform consoles, then drops those that
do not connect), so about 3.6 KiB of superseded variables pile up per
boot in the 256 KiB store. A warm restart keeps the store (the box
attaches it snapshot=on, so it lives as long as the QEMU process), and
at the 61st warm restart fewer than 33 KiB (PcdMaxAuthVariableSize) are
left, so at ReadyToBoot the variable driver reclaims (ReclaimForOS): it
rewrites the whole store through the fault-tolerant-write spare,
798,300 programmed bytes, 98.9 % of them 0xFF onto blocks just erased.
That held a warm restart 41-57 s in the firmware, before OpenCore
started, on an idle host, and for minutes on a busy one.

What it changes: QemuFlashWrite compares the buffer with what the flash
already holds and programs only the runs of bytes that differ, each run
through at most 16 unchanged bytes, with the same two writes per byte as
before, and returns the device to read-array mode after each run, as it
did after each call. A byte left alone holds the value programming it
would have stored, so the flash ends exactly as before. The comparison
reads the array, which the device returns only in read-array mode, and
an erase leaves it in status mode, where a read returns the status
register: so QemuFlashEraseBlock now ends with the read-array command
too. Nothing else in the driver, the FVB protocol or the variable driver
changes.

Upstream has no such change (tianocore/edk2 history of this file to
2026-10-25), and QEMU still writes per programmed byte.

Applied by internal/firmware's OVMF build after 0005, unless QemuFlash.c
already carries this file's marker comment. Its hunks keep upstream's
CRLF line endings.

diff --git a/OvmfPkg/QemuFlashFvbServicesRuntimeDxe/QemuFlash.c b/OvmfPkg/QemuFlashFvbServicesRuntimeDxe/QemuFlash.c
--- a/OvmfPkg/QemuFlashFvbServicesRuntimeDxe/QemuFlash.c
+++ b/OvmfPkg/QemuFlashFvbServicesRuntimeDxe/QemuFlash.c
@@ -194,6 +194,8 @@ QemuFlashWrite (
 {
   volatile UINT8  *Ptr;
   UINTN           Loop;
+  UINTN           End;
+  UINTN           Same;
 
   //
   // Only write to the first 64k. We don't bother saving the FTW Spare
@@ -203,22 +205,50 @@ QemuFlashWrite (
     return EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER;
   }
 
-  //
-  // Program flash
-  //
-  Ptr = QemuFlashPtr (Lba, Offset);
-  for (Loop = 0; Loop < *NumBytes; Loop++) {
-    QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr, WRITE_BYTE_CMD);
-    QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr, Buffer[Loop]);
-
-    Ptr++;
+  if (*NumBytes == 0) {
+    return EFI_SUCCESS;
   }
 
   //
-  // Restore flash to read mode
+  // packer-plugin-macosx: assets/firmware/patches/0006-ovmf-program-only-the-flash-bytes-that-change.patch
+  // Program only the bytes that change. QEMU's pflash traps both writes
+  // of every programmed byte and writes its backing file once per byte,
+  // so a variable reclaim, which rewrites the whole store through the
+  // FTW spare, some 800,000 bytes, nearly all 0xFF onto erased blocks,
+  // held a boot 41-57 s. A byte that already holds its value is left
+  // alone, which leaves the flash exactly as programming it would. The
+  // comparison reads the array, which the device returns only in
+  // read-array mode: this function and QemuFlashEraseBlock both leave it
+  // there. Runs of changed bytes are programmed together, through up to
+  // 16 unchanged ones, so each run leaves read-array mode once.
   //
-  if (*NumBytes > 0) {
-    QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr - 1, READ_ARRAY_CMD);
+  Ptr  = QemuFlashPtr (Lba, Offset);
+  Loop = 0;
+  while (Loop < *NumBytes) {
+    if (Ptr[Loop] == Buffer[Loop]) {
+      Loop++;
+      continue;
+    }
+
+    Same = 0;
+    for (End = Loop + 1; End < *NumBytes && Same < 16; End++) {
+      Same = (Ptr[End] == Buffer[End]) ? Same + 1 : 0;
+    }
+
+    End -= Same;
+
+    //
+    // Program flash
+    //
+    for ( ; Loop < End; Loop++) {
+      QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr + Loop, WRITE_BYTE_CMD);
+      QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr + Loop, Buffer[Loop]);
+    }
+
+    //
+    // Restore flash to read mode
+    //
+    QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr + End - 1, READ_ARRAY_CMD);
   }
 
   return EFI_SUCCESS;
@@ -244,6 +274,12 @@ QemuFlashEraseBlock (
   Ptr = QemuFlashPtr (Lba, 0);
   QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr, BLOCK_ERASE_CMD);
   QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr, BLOCK_ERASE_CONFIRM_CMD);
+
+  //
+  // packer-plugin-macosx: assets/firmware/patches/0006 (see QemuFlashWrite)
+  // Restore flash to read mode: QemuFlashWrite reads the array.
+  //
+  QemuFlashPtrWrite (Ptr, READ_ARRAY_CMD);
   return EFI_SUCCESS;
 }
 
